0.01: Consolidated production release. Replaces the previous apps/orbit implementation with the tested Sun-Earth-Moon + integrated 5-week calendar build; all app-owned names use orbit. Standard Settings > Apps > orbit now includes Place/Manual/GPS location modes, with all 47 Japanese prefectural capitals and world national capitals in Place mode. GPS is strictly on-demand inside Settings and is powered off after fix/cancel/exit; the runtime clock never enables GPS. Also includes calendar/holiday settings, yearly holiday bit caches, editable anniversaries/exceptional holidays, yellow selected-date blinking, outside-grid double-tap clearing, and no development timing/tap display.
0.02: Rebalance the main orbit display for the 176x176 screen: Sun radius 6-15, Earth 40-45, Moon 14-16 (target/default 8/42/15), and lunar-orbit radius uses a practical dynamic minimum Earth+Moon+4 instead of the old hidden Earth+3*Moon clamp. Stored and rendered Moon-orbit values now agree. Add a 400 ms tap discriminator on the orbit face: single tap opens the calendar, double tap opens orbit settings. Add an explicit "Exit to orbit" settings item; when settings is opened from orbit, Back also returns to orbit. GPS remains settings-only and on demand.
0.03: Fix an occasional stale previous-date yellow selection in the integrated calendar by fully repainting the calendar body whenever a double-tap moves or clears the selected date, then starting only the new selection blink. Restore View side = North/South in orbit settings. South view mirrors hour-angle and lunar-orbit handedness, uses a Southern Hemisphere land set (South America, southern Africa, Australia, Madagascar, New Zealand), uses an Antarctic polar cap, mirrors longitude projection, and flips declination sign for the day/night terminator.
      Expand Place selection for wide countries that use multiple civil-time standards. United States, Canada, Russia, Australia, Brazil, Mexico, Indonesia and Mongolia now offer representative cities across their major time-zone regions. Single-capital countries remain unchanged. Settings stores the chosen city index and preserves it by city name when reopened.
      Before a calendar single-tap returns to Orbit, stop the yellow selection timer and repaint the selected cell normally, then hand the selected date to Orbit on the next event-loop turn. This prevents a previous double-tap highlight from being left on the calendar framebuffer. Expand Japan Place settings to Prefecture -> Municipality using a compact per-prefecture random-access data file; only the selected prefecture's list is parsed into RAM.
      Simplify location handling to one canonical coordinate pair (manualLat/manualLon). Orbit runtime reads only those coordinates and then drops its config object. Manual edits write them directly; a successful GPS fix overwrites them and immediately powers GPS off; Place mode lazy-loads location tables only while browsing, reads only the selected Japanese prefecture's municipality slice, commits the chosen coordinates into manualLat/manualLon only on "Use place", and releases municipality/place references plus the orbitloc module cache when leaving Place. Legacy lat/lon fields are migrated once and deleted.
      Restore the location-source UI as three explicit menu entries instead of a mode spinner: Place name, Manual, and GPS. Place name alone loads country/prefecture/municipality data; Manual and GPS remain independent and do not load the place database. This also makes the previously requested place-name mode unambiguous on the watch.
0.04: Stable release of the current Orbit feature set. No runtime behavior or data format is intentionally changed from 0.033. Code comments are cleaned up to describe the present implementation rather than development-history versions, README wording is synchronized with the country/place display, and the release is promoted as the stable baseline for future bug fixes.
      Rebuild japan-municipalities.dat from the license-cleared MLIT National Land Numerical Information P05 2022 (CC BY 4.0) lineage using the MIT-licensed jp-address-search municipality-code transformation, preserving Orbit display labels while replacing coordinates, rounding them to 4 decimals, and regenerating jpidx byte ranges. Rewrite README in Japanese around purpose, usage, program structure, references, provenance, transformations and licenses. No clock/calendar runtime logic is changed.
0.05: Refine the stable Orbit release for normal daily use and named-place civil time. Use native Bangle.js top-bar widgets for battery/Bluetooth status and keep Orbit date/time clear of the widget bar. Make the active location source explicit and persistent; Place name shows country/place, Manual shows coordinates, and GPS shows a satellite mark with coordinates. Improve place-selection persistence and wrist-view backlight wake behaviour. Add independent Date position / Time position choices (Header, Top left, Below Sun) and separate on-face font-size controls. Darken the Earth land colour for improved contrast.
      On Bangle.js 2, keep the memory-in-pixel LCD powered during idle and turn off only the backlight; stop overriding the standard lock event so normal button/twist wake behaviour remains available and the clock cannot become stuck on a black display.
      Separate astronomical geometry from civil-time display: Sun/Earth/Moon geometry and solar hour angle are calculated from absolute UTC time, so changing timezone or daylight-saving time cannot rotate the celestial display. Add Time source = Bangle / Place, with Bangle as the default. Place time converts UTC inside Orbit using the selected named place's standard UTC offset and compact shared DST rule without changing the Bangle.js system timezone. Add compact timezone/DST data for the existing world-place table, including fractional-hour offsets and shared regional DST rules; all Japanese municipalities use the common UTC+9 / no-DST setting.
